S. Ermon (2020a) Denoising diffusion implicit models . arXiv preprint arXiv:2010.02502 . Cited by: §4.3 . Y. Song, J. Sohl-Dickstein, D. P. Kingma, A. Kumar, S. Ermon, and B. Poole (2020b) Score-based generative modeling through stochastic differential equations . arXiv preprint arXiv:2011.13456 . Cited by: §2 . Y. Takagi and S. Nishimoto (2023) High-resolution image reconstruction with latent diffusion models from human brain activity . In Proceedings of the IEEE/CVF conference on computer vision and pattern recognition , pp. 14453–14463 . Cited by: §1 , §1 , §2 , §2 . J. Tang, A. LeBel, S. Jain, and A. G. Huth (2023) Semantic reconstruction of continuous language from non-invasive brain recordings . Nature Neuroscience 26 ( 5 ), pp. 858–866 . Cited by: §2 . G. Tuckute, J. Feather, D. Boebinger, and J. H. McDermott (2023) Many but not all deep neural network audio models capture brain responses and exhibit correspondence between model stages and brain regions . Plos Biology 21 ( 12 ), pp. e3002366 . Cited by: §2 , §2 . G. Tzanetakis and P. Cook (2002) Musical genre classification of audio signals . IEEE Transactions on speech and audio processing 10 ( 5 ), pp. 293–302 . Cited by: Appendix G , Appendix I , §1 , §5 , §6 . Y. Wu, K. Chen, T. Zhang, Y. Hui, T. Berg-Kirkpatrick, and S. Dubnov (2023) Large-scale contrastive language-audio pretraining with feature fusion and keyword-to-caption augmentation . In ICASSP 2023-2023 IEEE International Conference on Acoustics, Speech and Signal Processing (ICASSP) , pp. 1–5 . Cited by: Appendix C , §3 , §4.1 . Y. Yuan, H. Liu, X. Liu, Q. Huang, M. D. Plumbley, and W. Wang (2024) Retrieval-augmented text-to-audio generation . In ICASSP 2024-2024 IEEE International Conference on Acoustics, Speech and Signal Processing (ICASSP) , pp. 581–585 . Cited by: §1 , §2 . Appendix A Code and Reproducibility For reproducibility, we release the full codebase, including preprocessing, the fMRI-to-CLAP decoder, retrieval, exemplar anchoring, and evaluation, in a public repository: TBA The repository documents the environment, checkpoints, and configuration files needed to reproduce every table and figure in this document. Appendix B Use of Large Language Models Large language models were used only to improve grammar and clarity in author-written text. Appendix C Implementation Details Decoder. The contrastive decoder f θ f_{\\theta} is a multilayer perceptron mapping the 2000 2000 selected voxels to the 512 512 -dimensional CLAP space. It is trained for 400 400 epochs with the InfoNCE objective (Oord et al. , 2018 ) at temperature τ = 0.07 \\tau=0.07 , using the Adam optimizer with weight decay; full layer widths, learning rate, and batch size are given in our released configuration. Voxels are selected by the relevance score of Eq. 5 ( K = 2000 K=2000 ), with a haemodynamic lag of ℓ = 3 \\ell=3 TRs and a temporal window of w = 8 w=8 TRs. Motion correction is rigid-body, performed in native space with ANTsPy (Avants et al. , 2011 ) . Generators. All three generators are frozen and used at their public checkpoints: AudioLDM ( cvssp/audioldm-s-full v2 ) (Liu et al. , 2023a ) , TangoFlux ( declare-lab/TangoFlux ) (Hung et al. , 2024 ) , and MusicGen ( facebook/musicgen-melody ) (Copet et al. , 2023 ) . For anchored generation we sweep the strength grid s ∈ { 0.15 , 0.25 , 0.35 , 0.5 , 0.65 , 0.8 , 1.0 } s\\in\\{0.15,0.25,0.35,0.5,0.65,0.8,1.0\\} ; generation step counts and guidance scales are listed in the released configuration. Identification uses the LAION CLAP checkpoint laion/clap-htsat-unfused (Wu et al. , 2023 ) , which is distinct from the CLAP variant used as the decoder target. Appendix D Decoder Baselines The contrastive decoder is compared against ridge, linear, and MLP regressors trained to predict the CLAP target directly. As summarized in Figure 8 , the contrastive decoder attains the best 10 10 -way identification despite the lowest dimension-wise correlation with the target embedding (Pearson r = 0.20 r=0.20 , versus 0.68 0.68 – 0.71 0.71 for the regression decoders). This apparent paradox reflects the training objective: InfoNCE optimizes the relative cosine geometry that N N -way identification actually measures, whereas ridge/linear/MLP minimize dimension-wise error, which need not preserve nearest-neighbor rankings. Because retrieval and identification both operate on cosine similarity, the contrastive criterion is the better match to the downstream task. Figure 8: Decoder 10-way identification (5-subj mean). Table 2: Identification at N = 2 , 5 , 10 N\\!=\\!2,5,10 for every generator × \\times arm (direct, RAG, retrieval; n = 300 n{=}300 , chance 1 / N 1/N ). RAG matches the retrieval bound for the latent-trajectory generators but not MusicGen. † \\dagger : only 10 10 -way was aggregated for AudioLDM (Table 1 ). Generator Arm 2-way 5-way 10-way AudioLDM direct † \\dagger † \\dagger 0.14 RAG † \\dagger † \\dagger 0.43 retrieval † \\dagger † \\dagger 0.40 TangoFlux direct 0.515 0.249 0.140 RAG 0.785 0.555 0.403 retrieval 0.792 0.554 0.406 MusicGen direct 0.590 0.310 0.180 RAG 0.615 0.336 0.201 retrieval 0.796 0.553 0.402 Appendix E Per-Subject Results The decoder is reliable across all five subjects. Table 3 reports per-subject identification for the contrastive decoder ( 60 60 test clips each): every subject exceeds chance at all N N , with a mean 10 10 -way accuracy of 0.434 0.434 (chance 0.10 0.10 ) and a best subject (sub-003) at 0.532 0.532 . Table 4 reports the peak voxel relevance ( max ⁡ | corr | \\max|\\mathrm{corr}| with the CLAP target) per subject, ranging from 0.425 0.425 (sub-001) to 0.599 0.599 (sub-003); the ordering tracks identification accuracy, indicating that subjects with stronger auditory-cortex coupling are decoded more reliably. Bilateral superior-temporal-gyrus localization is consistent across subjects (Figure 3 ), and the single-subject map for sub-001 is shown in the main text. Table 3: Per-subject identification of the contrastive decoder ( 60 60 test clips each; chance 1 / N 1/N ). All five subjects exceed chance at every N N , confirming the decoder is reliable across subjects. Subject 2-way 5-way 10-way 50-way Pearson sub-001 0.782 0.555 0.393 0.121 0.177 sub-002 0.820 0.591 0.421 0.145 0.189 sub-003 0.836 0.651 0.532 0.206 0.234 sub-004 0.790 0.560 0.437 0.173 0.174 sub-005 0.794 0.546 0.388 0.154 0.233 mean 0.804 0.581 0.434 0.159 0.201 Table 4: Per-subject peak voxel relevance (max | corr | |\\mathrm{corr}| with the CLAP target), indexing decoder strength in auditory cortex for each subject. Subject sub-001 sub-002 sub-003 sub-004 sub-005 peak | | corr | | 0.425 0.496 0.599 0.454 0.485 Table 5: Audio realism ( n = 300 n{=}300 ; lower is better): FAD (VGGish) with FD and KL (PANNs Cnn14). RAG reduces all three for AudioLDM and TangoFlux toward the real-audio retrieval reference, while MusicGen changes little. Generator Arm FAD FD KL AudioLDM direct 13.49 79.40 3.08 RAG 1.25 17.23 1.07 retrieval 0.89 16.40 1.05 TangoFlux direct 7.89 59.11 2.24 RAG 2.36 21.49 1.01 retrieval 0.89 16.40 1.05 MusicGen direct 8.06 52.99 1.85 RAG 6.21 59.32 1.82 retrieval 0.89 17.55 1.08 Table 6: Novelty ( 1 − cos 1-\\cos to the nearest training clip) and pairwise diversity for the TangoFlux arms. RAG is far more novel than verbatim retrieval ( ≈ 0 {\\approx}0 ) while staying below unconstrained direct generation. Arm novelty diversity retrieval 0.06 0.42 RAG (ours) 0.18 0.39 direct (prior) 0.33 0.48 Appendix F Memory-Bank Size Ablation Appendix G Genre Confusion To characterize the residual errors, each generated clip is labelled by the genre of its nearest ground-truth neighbour in CLAP space (leave-one-out). Figure 9 shows the resulting confusion matrix for the RAG arm (TangoFlux): top-1 genre accuracy is 0.33 0.33 , or 3.3 × 3.3\\times the 0.10 0.10 chance level. Well-separated genres are recovered most often (classical 0.63 0.63 , jazz 0.53 0.53 , hip hop/pop 0.43 0.43 ), while the errors concentrate among the acoustically overlapping GTZAN (Tzanetakis and Cook, 2002 ) genres (metal, rock, blues) that are confusable even for audio-only classifiers. The confusions are musically coherent (e.g., disco ↔ \\leftrightarrow pop ↔ \\leftrightarrow reggae, rock ↔ \\leftrightarrow disco), indicating that reconstruction errors preserve broad timbral and rhythmic structure rather than being random. Figure 9: Genre confusion for the RAG arm (TangoFlux). Appendix H SDEdit Strength Sweep Table 7 details the dependence of 10 10 -way identification on the anchoring strength s s that underlies Figure 4 . For AudioLDM, identification is highest at low strength ( 0.49 0.49 at s = 0.2 s=0.2 ) and decreases monotonically toward the 0.10 0.10 chance level as s → 1 s\\to 1 , crossing the retrieval bound within the working range. TangoFlux was evaluated at its operating point s = 0.4 s=0.4 , where it reaches 0.40 0.40 . Realism is stable across the anchored range: AudioLDM FAD is 1.14 1.14 , 1.12 1.12 , and 1.25 1.25 at s = 0.15 s=0.15 , 0.2 0.2 , and 0.25 0.25 respectively, so faithfulness can be tuned over this interval without degrading audio quality. We therefore adopt s ≈ 0.25 s\\approx 0.25 – 0.40 0.40 as the default operating region. Table 7: Ten-way identification versus anchoring strength s s (AudioLDM sweep; TangoFlux at its operating point). Accuracy is highest at low s s and decays to the 0.10 0.10 chance level as s → 1 s\\!\\to\\!1 ; retr. = retrieval bound, direct = prior-only. Generator s = 0.2 s{=}0.2 0.25 0.3 0.4 0.5 retr. direct AudioLDM 0.49 0.43 0.39 0.30 0.14 0.40 0.14 TangoFlux — — — 0.40 — 0.40 0.14 We tested whether RAG-Audio’s advantage grows as the retrieval memory shrinks, hypothesizing graceful degradation. The data did not support this. Table 8 report AudioLDM RAG ( s = 0.2 s=0.2 ) against retrieval FAD at bank sizes 30 30 , 120 120 , and 480 480 . At reduced bank sizes the estimates are noisy ( n = 60 n=60 pairs versus 300 300 at the full bank), and RAG tracks retrieval within that noise rather than separating from it as the bank shrinks. We report this as an honest negative result: the benefit of anchoring does not increase under retrieval scarcity in this dataset. Table 8: Memory-bank size ablation: AudioLDM RAG ( s = 0.2 s{=}0.2 ) vs. retrieval FAD at bank sizes 30 / 120 / 480 30/120/480 ( n = 60 n{=}60 pairs, noisy). RAG tracks retrieval within noise and does not degrade gracefully as the bank shrinks. bank 30 bank 120 full (480) RAG FAD 1.43 1.88 1.12 retrieval FAD 1.90 1.28 0.89 Appendix I Dataset and Reproducibility We use the Brain2Music fMRI dataset (Denk et al. , 2023 ) , released as OpenNeuro ds003720 (Nakai et al. , 2022 ) , obtained via DataLad ( datalad get on the *_bold.nii files); the auditory stimuli are drawn from the ten GTZAN genres (Tzanetakis and Cook, 2002 ) . We follow the official subject-wise split of 480 480 training and 60 60 test clips per subject. Preprocessing applies ANTsPy rigid motion correction in native space (Avants et al. , 2011 ) , a lag of 3 3 TRs and window of 8 8 TRs, and top- 2000 2000 voxel selection by Eq. 5 . Auditory-cortex overlap is computed against the Harvard-Oxford atlas (Desikan et al. , 2006 ) . All runs use a single GPU per job; we release seeds, code, and configurations. Experimental support, please view the build logs for errors. Generated by L A T E xml . Instructions for reporting errors We are continuing to improve HTML versions of papers, and your feedback helps enhance accessibility and mobile support. To report errors in the HTML that will help us improve conversion and rendering, choose any of the methods listed below: Click the \"Report Issue\" ( ) button, located in the page header. Tip: You can select the relevant text first, to include it in your report. Our team has already identified the following issues . We appreciate your time reviewing and reporting rendering errors we may not have found yet. Your efforts will help us improve the HTML v