ll three for AudioLDM and TangoFlux toward the real-audio retrieval reference, while MusicGen changes little. Generator Arm FAD FD KL AudioLDM direct 13.49 79.40 3.08 RAG 1.25 17.23 1.07 retrieval 0.89 16.40 1.05 TangoFlux direct 7.89 59.11 2.24 RAG 2.36 21.49 1.01 retrieval 0.89 16.40 1.05 MusicGen direct 8.06 52.99 1.85 RAG 6.21 59.32 1.82 retrieval 0.89 17.55 1.08 Table 6: Novelty ( 1 − cos 1-\\cos to the nearest training clip) and pairwise diversity for the TangoFlux arms. RAG is far more novel than verbatim retrieval ( ≈ 0 {\\approx}0 ) while staying below unconstrained direct generation. Arm novelty diversity retrieval 0.06 0.42 RAG (ours) 0.18 0.39 direct (prior) 0.33 0.48 Appendix F Memory-Bank Size Ablation Appendix G Genre Confusion To characterize the residual errors, each generated clip is labelled by the genre of its nearest ground-truth neighbour in CLAP space (leave-one-out). Figure 9 shows the resulting confusion matrix for the RAG arm (TangoFlux): top-1 genre accuracy is 0.33 0.33 , or 3.3 × 3.3\\times the 0.10 0.10 chance level. Well-separated genres are recovered most often (classical 0.63 0.63 , jazz 0.53 0.53 , hip hop/pop 0.43 0.43 ), while the errors concentrate among the acoustically overlapping GTZAN (Tzanetakis and Cook, 2002 ) genres (metal, rock, blues) that are confusable even for audio-only classifiers. The confusions are musically coherent (e.g., disco ↔ \\leftrightarrow pop ↔ \\leftrightarrow reggae, rock ↔ \\leftrightarrow disco), indicating that reconstruction errors preserve broad timbral and rhythmic structure rather than being random. Figure 9: Genre confusion for the RAG arm (TangoFlux). Appendix H SDEdit Strength Sweep Table 7 details the dependence of 10 10 -way identification on the anchoring strength s s that underlies Figure 4 . For AudioLDM, identification is highest at low strength ( 0.49 0.49 at s = 0.2 s=0.2 ) and decreases monotonically toward the 0.10 0.10 chance level as s → 1 s\\to 1 , crossing the retrieval bound within the working range. TangoFlux was evaluated at its operating point s = 0.4 s=0.4 , where it reaches 0.40 0.40 . Realism is stable across the anchored range: AudioLDM FAD is 1.14 1.14 , 1.12 1.12 , and 1.25 1.25 at s = 0.15 s=0.15 , 0.2 0.2 , and 0.25 0.25 respectively, so faithfulness can be tuned over this interval without degrading audio quality. We therefore adopt s ≈ 0.25 s\\approx 0.25 – 0.40 0.40 as the default operating region. Table 7: Ten-way identification versus anchoring strength s s (AudioLDM sweep; TangoFlux at its operating point). Accuracy is highest at low s s and decays to the 0.10 0.10 chance level as s → 1 s\\!\\to\\!1 ; retr. = retrieval bound, direct = prior-only. Generator s = 0.2 s{=}0.2 0.25 0.3 0.4 0.5 retr. direct AudioLDM 0.49 0.43 0.39 0.30 0.14 0.40 0.14 TangoFlux — — — 0.40 — 0.40 0.14 We tested whether RAG-Audio’s advantage grows as the retrieval memory shrinks, hypothesizing graceful degradation. The data did not support this. Table 8 report AudioLDM RAG ( s = 0.2 s=0.2 ) against retrieval FAD at bank sizes 30 30 , 120 120 , and 480 480 . At reduced bank sizes the estimates are noisy ( n = 60 n=60 pairs versus 300 300 at the full bank), and RAG tracks retrieval within that noise rather than separating from it as the bank shrinks. We report this as an honest negative result: the benefit of anchoring does not increase under retrieval scarcity in this dataset. Table 8: Memory-bank size ablation: AudioLDM RAG ( s = 0.2 s{=}0.2 ) vs. retrieval FAD at bank sizes 30 / 120 / 480 30/120/480 ( n = 60 n{=}60 pairs, noisy). RAG tracks retrieval within noise and does not degrade gracefully as the bank shrinks. bank 30 bank 120 full (480) RAG FAD 1.43 1.88 1.12 retrieval FAD 1.90 1.28 0.89 Appendix I Dataset and Reproducibility We use the Brain2Music fMRI dataset (Denk et al. , 2023 ) , released as OpenNeuro ds003720 (Nakai et al. , 2022 ) , obtained via DataLad ( datalad get on the *_bold.nii files); the auditory stimuli are drawn from the ten GTZAN genres (Tzanetakis and Cook, 2002 ) . We follow the official subject-wise split of 480 480 training and 60 60 test clips per subject. Preprocessing applies ANTsPy rigid motion correction in native space (Avants et al. , 2011 ) , a lag of 3 3 TRs and window of 8 8 TRs, and top- 2000 2000 voxel selection by Eq. 5 . Auditory-cortex overlap is computed against the Harvard-Oxford atlas (Desikan et al. , 2006 ) . All runs use a single GPU per job; we release seeds, code, and configurations. Experimental support, please view the build logs for errors. Generated by L A T E xml . Instructions for reporting errors We are continuing to improve HTML versions of papers, and your feedback helps enhance accessibility and mobile support. To report errors in the HTML that will help us improve conversion and rendering, choose any of the methods listed below: Click the \"Report Issue\" ( ) button, located in the page header. Tip: You can select the relevant text first, to include it in your report. Our team has already identified the following issues . We appreciate your time reviewing and reporting rendering errors we may not have found yet. Your efforts will help us improve the HTML versions for all readers, because disability should not be a barrier to accessing research. Thank you for your continued support in championing open access for all. Have a free development cycle? Help support accessibility at arXiv! Our collaborators at LaTeXML maintain a list of packages that need conversion , and welcome developer contributions . We gratefully acknowledge support from our major funders , member institutions , , and all contributors. Major funding support from\n", "numLines": 2, "startLine": 1, "totalLines": 2}}}
